-- js-effects.rofl — THE FIFTH LAYER, AND THE ONLY ONE WITH A JOIN.
--
-- facts/js-effects.rofl declares `layer(effect)`, the Koka taxonomy and the
-- hundred verdicts. This file is the ORDER those verdicts are about, the rules
-- that put a corpus node into it, and the audits that keep both honest.
--
-- THREE ORDERS NOW LIVE IN THIS TREE AND THEY ARE DELIBERATELY NOT MERGED.
-- The next reader will want to unify them, so the refusal is written here
-- rather than left to be rediscovered:
--
--   ES RELEASES        `release`, `includes`, `provides`, `reaches` in the era
--                      layer — a TOTAL order in time, generated from
--                      TypeScript's own `/// <reference lib=` lines. A later
--                      edition contains an earlier one. NO JOIN: "the join of
--                      es2015 and es2020" is not a question anybody asks; the
--                      question is `env_has`, which is a comparison.
--   RUNTIME VERSIONS   `runtime_includes(node20, node18)`, with the named
--                      bridge `provides_release(node20, es2022)` into the scale
--                      above. A subkind order, approved by the owner
--                      2026-09-09. NO JOIN either, and for a sharper reason:
--                      node20 and a browser have no common runtime, and
--                      inventing one to make the order a lattice would be
--                      manufacturing a term.
--   THE EFFECT LATTICE this file. It HAS a join and a meet, and it needs them,
--                      because the two questions the layer is asked are exactly
--                      a join (what is a body's effect given its statements')
--                      and a comparison (is this call at least as effectful as
--                      that one).
--
-- They are three orders over three different carriers and the only thing they
-- share is the word. Merging them would mean asking what `es2020 join
-- write<local>` is, which is not a question about anything.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 1. THE ORDER, AND IT IS DERIVED FROM THE ROWS RATHER THAN TYPED OUT
--
-- Koka's aliases are DEFINITIONS, so `eff_row` is closed over `eff_alias`
-- instead of `pure`'s two labels being written down a second time. That is what
-- makes `join(div, exn) = pure` a derivation and not a coincidence between two
-- hand-written tables.
eff_row(A, L, H) :- eff_alias(A, P), eff_row(P, L, H).

-- INCLUSION, VIA ITS OWN NEGATION. `A <= B` when no label of A is missing from
-- B — which is subset inclusion, so reflexivity and transitivity come from the
-- rows rather than from arms somebody has to remember to write. `total` has no
-- rows, so it lacks nothing and is below everything: the bottom is a
-- consequence of the encoding rather than a declaration.
eff_lacks(A, B) :- eff_name(B), eff_row(A, L, H), not eff_row(B, L, H).
eff_leq(A, B)   :- eff_name(A), eff_name(B), not eff_lacks(A, B).
eff_lt(A, B)    :- eff_leq(A, B), not eff_leq(B, A).

-- THE JOIN AND THE MEET, AS LEAST UPPER AND GREATEST LOWER BOUNDS OVER THE
-- NAMED FAMILY. The join of two named rows is the least NAME above their union;
-- it is not the union itself, because the fourteen landmarks are not closed
-- under union and naming every subset would be sixty-four rows nobody reads.
--
-- THAT IS SOUND EXACTLY WHEN THE FAMILY IS CLOSED UNDER INTERSECTION and
-- contains the full row — a Moore family — and that is a THEOREM about this
-- table rather than a hope about it. The four ways it can fail are the four
-- audits below, and they are the acceptance for this section.
eff_ub(A, B, C)       :- eff_leq(A, C), eff_leq(B, C).
eff_ub_lower(A, B, C) :- eff_ub(A, B, D), eff_lt(D, C).
eff_join(A, B, C)     :- eff_ub(A, B, C), not eff_ub_lower(A, B, C).
eff_has_join(A, B)    :- eff_join(A, B, _).

eff_lb(A, B, C)        :- eff_leq(C, A), eff_leq(C, B).
eff_lb_higher(A, B, C) :- eff_lb(A, B, D), eff_lt(C, D).
eff_meet(A, B, C)      :- eff_lb(A, B, C), not eff_lb_higher(A, B, C).
eff_has_meet(A, B)     :- eff_meet(A, B, _).

-- THE FOUR WAYS THIS IS NOT A LATTICE. `missing` is a pair with no bound at all
-- and `ambiguous` is a pair with two incomparable minimal ones; either makes
-- the join a partial or a multi-valued function, and both are silent otherwise
-- — the join relation simply answers nothing, or answers twice, and every
-- reader downstream sees a smaller or a doubled set with nothing saying why.
join_missing[audit](A, B)         :- eff_name(A), eff_name(B), not eff_has_join(A, B).
join_ambiguous[audit](A, B, C, D) :- eff_join(A, B, C), eff_join(A, B, D), C != D.
meet_missing[audit](A, B)         :- eff_name(A), eff_name(B), not eff_has_meet(A, B).
meet_ambiguous[audit](A, B, C, D) :- eff_meet(A, B, C), eff_meet(A, B, D), C != D.

-- ...AND THE BOTTOM AND THE TOP, AS SETS RATHER THAN AS TWO CLAIMED NAMES. A
-- lattice with a bottom and a top is what the founding finding asked for, so
-- both are DERIVED and the test asserts the derived set is `{total}` and
-- `{top}`. Naming them in a fact would have made the claim unfalsifiable.
eff_not_top(T) :- eff_name(T), eff_name(M), not eff_leq(M, T).
eff_not_bot(B) :- eff_name(B), eff_name(M), not eff_leq(B, M).
eff_top(T)     :- eff_name(T), not eff_not_top(T).
eff_bot(B)     :- eff_name(B), not eff_not_bot(B).

-- ORDINARY HYGIENE OF AN AUTHORED TABLE, each direction a positive row. A
-- misspelt alias is the dangerous one: it silently gives a NAME the empty row,
-- which makes it the bottom, which makes every comparison against it true.
eff_row_unknown[audit](N)      :- eff_row(N, _, _), not eff_name(N).
eff_label_unknown[audit](L, H) :- eff_row(_, L, H), not eff_label(L, H).
eff_alias_unknown[audit](A, P) :- eff_alias(A, P), not eff_name(P).
eff_alias_unnamed[audit](A)    :- eff_alias(A, _), not eff_name(A).
eff_name_empty[audit](N)       :- eff_name(N), not eff_row(N, _, _), N != total.
eff_heap_unknown[audit](H)     :- eff_label(_, H), not eff_heap(H).
-- a label no name reaches is a label the lattice cannot express
eff_label_unnamed[audit](L, H) :- eff_label(L, H), not eff_row(_, L, H).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 2. WHAT A NODE CONTRIBUTES — THE SEEDS
--
-- `eff_here[flow](Node, Label, Heap)` is one label a node contributes when it
-- is REACHED. The join is not performed here: two rules with the same head are
-- a union, which is what a join over a row IS, so the row of a function is the
-- set of `eff_here` labels of everything that runs in it. That is the whole
-- reason the carrier is a set of labels rather than a name — a name would have
-- needed a join operation at every step, and a set needs none.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- EXN. Seeded from a `throw` that its own function does not catch — the same
-- seed `throws_outright[code]` uses, so the exn projection of this layer can be
-- compared row for row against `may_throw`. What a try discharges through is
-- `catches_via` in rules/js-controlflow.rofl: the handler, and never a
-- finalizer alone. That guard sat here, on this relation only, from 2026-09-09
-- until the corpus gained a site (`leaky` in alpha.mjs) and it moved to the
-- source on 2026-09-11.
eff_catch_here[code](N) :- caught_here[code](N).

-- ...the two arms of ONE relation, so that "how many try statements lack a
-- handler" is a query with a control that is the same literal with ONE
-- CONSTANT swapped — the form a witness demands.
eff_try_arm[flow](T, handled)   :- ast_node[code](T, try_statement, _, _),
                                   ast_child[code](T, handler, 0, _).
eff_try_arm[flow](T, unhandled) :- ast_node[code](T, try_statement, _, _),
                                   not ast_child[code](T, handler, 0, _).

eff_here[flow](T, exn, none) :- ast_node[code](T, throw_statement, _, _),
                                not eff_catch_here[code](T).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DIV. Two seeds, and the second is the one a loop-shaped rule would miss.
edb(eff_loop_kind).
eff_loop_kind(while_statement).
eff_loop_kind(do_while_statement).
eff_loop_kind(for_statement).
eff_loop_kind(for_in_statement).
eff_loop_kind(for_of_statement).

eff_here[flow](N, div, none) :- eff_loop_kind(K), ast_node[code](N, K, _, _).

-- RECURSION IS DIVERGENCE TOO, and it has no syntax. A call that can come back
-- to the function it is written in may not terminate for exactly the reason a
-- `while (true)` may not, and nothing in the tree shows it. `eff_reaches` is
-- the transitive callee closure of `resolves`; the seed is a call site whose
-- callee reaches its own caller.
--
-- ORDERED FOR COST: `eff_calls` leads with `resolves`, which binds both ends,
-- so `nearest_v` is probed by the call site rather than enumerated.
eff_calls[code](F, G)   :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C), fn_node[code](G).
eff_reaches[code](F, G) :- eff_calls[code](F, G).
eff_reaches[code](F, H) :- eff_reaches[code](F, G), eff_calls[code](G, H).

eff_here[flow](C, div, none) :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C),
                                eff_reaches[code](G, F).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ALLOC. A fresh MUTABLE IDENTITY. `new_expression` is in this list although
-- its CELL is open: the allocation half is derived and complete, and what is
-- open is the constructor's effect, which is the ambient-surface question.
-- A rule may be right about half a kind while the cell stays honest about the
-- other half — that is what an open cell beside a firing rule means here.
edb(eff_alloc_kind).
eff_alloc_kind(object_expression).
eff_alloc_kind(array_expression).
eff_alloc_kind(reg_exp_literal).
eff_alloc_kind(function_expression).
eff_alloc_kind(arrow_function_expression).
eff_alloc_kind(class_expression).
eff_alloc_kind(new_expression).

eff_here[flow](N, alloc, none) :- eff_alloc_kind(K), ast_node[code](N, K, _, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- READ AND WRITE, AND THEIR HEAP.
--
-- THE HEAP IS DECIDED BY WHETHER THE VALUE LAYER CAN TRACE THE RECEIVER. If
-- `may_be_node[flow]` reaches an allocation for the object, the object is one
-- this program made and the heap is `local`; if it does not, the receiver came
-- from outside and the heap is `global`. The two arms are written as a
-- negation pair rather than as one arm with a default, so a receiver cannot
-- fall out of both and be silently effect-free.
eff_obj_traced[flow](M) :- member_node_v[flow](M), ast_child[code](M, object, 0, O),
                           may_be_node[flow](O, _).
eff_heap_of[flow](M, local)  :- member_node_v[flow](M), eff_obj_traced[flow](M).
eff_heap_of[flow](M, global) :- member_node_v[flow](M), not eff_obj_traced[flow](M).

eff_assign[code](A)           :- ast_node[code](A, assignment_expression, _, _).
eff_assign_target[code](A, L) :- eff_assign[code](A), ast_child[code](A, left, 0, L).
-- `plain_assign[flow]` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT IS NOT MINE. The value layer
-- already separates `=` from `+=` because a compound assignment evaluates to a
-- SUM and that layer knows nothing about sums; here the same relation separates
-- a write from a read-and-write, which is a second use for one distinction.
eff_compound[code](A)         :- eff_assign[code](A), not plain_assign[flow](A).
eff_member_target[code](L)    :- eff_assign_target[code](_, L), member_node_v[flow](L).
eff_name_target[code](L)      :- eff_assign_target[code](_, L), ast_name[code](L, _).

eff_here[flow](L, write, H) :- eff_member_target[code](L), eff_heap_of[flow](L, H).
eff_here[flow](L, read, H)  :- eff_compound[code](A), ast_child[code](A, left, 0, L),
                               eff_member_target[code](L), eff_heap_of[flow](L, H).
eff_here[flow](A, write, local) :- eff_assign_target[code](A, L), ast_name[code](L, _).

-- A MEMBER THAT IS NOT AN ASSIGNMENT TARGET IS A READ. This covers the whole
-- of `member_expression` and `optional_member_expression`, on both heaps, with
-- no residue — which is why those two cells are `handled` while
-- `call_expression` is not: reading `console.log` really is a `read<global>`
-- and the unknown is what the resulting function DOES.
eff_read_site[flow](M) :- member_node_v[flow](M), not eff_member_target[code](M).
eff_here[flow](M, read, H) :- eff_read_site[flow](M), eff_heap_of[flow](M, H).

-- ...AND THE TWO HALVES PARTITION THE MEMBER NODES, which is an IDENTITY and
-- therefore true of any corpus rather than of this one. The `never both` half
-- is this row; the `never neither` half is the sum, asserted in
-- test/js-effects.test.ts. A count of read sites could not tell a read the rule
-- MISSED from a write the rule called a read.
eff_member_both[audit](M) :- eff_read_site[flow](M), eff_member_target[code](M).

-- `x++` IS A READ AND A WRITE, on whichever of the two forms it takes.
eff_update_arg[code](U, X) :- ast_node[code](U, update_expression, _, _),
                              ast_child[code](U, argument, 0, X).
eff_here[flow](U, read, local)  :- eff_update_arg[code](U, X), ast_name[code](X, _).
eff_here[flow](U, write, local) :- eff_update_arg[code](U, X), ast_name[code](X, _).
eff_here[flow](U, read, H)      :- eff_update_arg[code](U, X), eff_heap_of[flow](X, H).
eff_here[flow](U, write, H)     :- eff_update_arg[code](U, X), eff_heap_of[flow](X, H).

-- READING A MUTABLE BINDING IS `read<local>` AND READING AN IMMUTABLE ONE IS
-- NOTHING. That is Koka's answer rather than a shortcut: `read<h>` is a read of
-- a CELL, and a binding nothing reassigns has no cell to read — it is a value.
-- So the mutable names are named first and the identifiers are probed by name.
--
-- ORDERED FOR COST, and this is the ordering `f_the_engine_has_no_join_planner`
-- was written about: `eff_mutable_name` is a handful of rows and binds both
-- Name and File, so `ident_in[code]` — which the store indexes on exactly that
-- pair — is a probe. Leading with the identifier would have enumerated every
-- identifier in the corpus for each mutable name.
eff_mutable_name[code](Name, File) :- assigns[code](Name, _, File).
eff_mutable_name[code](Name, File) :- eff_update_arg[code](U, X), ast_name[code](X, Name),
                                      ast_node[code](U, _, File, _).
eff_here[flow](N, read, local) :- eff_mutable_name[code](Name, File),
                                  ident_in[code](N, Name, File),
                                  not eff_name_target[code](N).

-- WHICH LABELS A CORPUS ACTUALLY EXERCISES. A POSITIVE relation and not an
-- audit, because two of the eight are empty on an honest checkout today and a
-- gate red on an honest checkout gets switched off. The two are empty for
-- DIFFERENT reasons and the difference is the whole value of the row:
--
--   write/global   HAS a seed and no site. Nothing in this corpus assigns to a
--                  member of a receiver the value layer cannot trace.
--   ndet           has NO SEED AT ALL, and cannot have one. Nothing in the
--                  LANGUAGE is nondeterministic; `Math.random`, `Date.now` and
--                  `crypto.getRandomValues` are ambient members, so `ndet` is
--                  derivable exactly when `ambient_effect` exists. It is the
--                  sharpest single statement of what this layer is waiting for.
eff_label_seen[flow](L, H)   :- eff_here[flow](_, L, H).
eff_label_unseen[flow](L, H) :- eff_label(L, H), not eff_label_seen[flow](L, H).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 3. THE JOIN OVER A BODY, AND THE CLOSURE OVER THE CALL GRAPH
--
-- `eff_latent[flow](F, Label, Heap)` is a function's row: what it may do when
-- called. The first arm is the join over its own body — every `eff_here` label
-- of a node whose nearest value context is F — and the join is the union two
-- arms with one head already are.
eff_latent[flow](F, L, H) :- eff_here[flow](N, L, H), nearest_v[flow](F, N).

-- A HANDLER REMOVES A LABEL FROM A ROW, PER LABEL. This is the piece of an
-- effect system a flat list of labels cannot express, and writing it as
-- `not eff_catch_here(C)` on the whole arm — which is what `may_throw` does,
-- correctly, because `may_throw` carries only one label — would have made a
-- `try` around a call swallow the callee's WRITES as well as its throws.
edb(eff_discharges).
eff_discharges(catch_clause, exn).
eff_discharged_at[code](C, L) :- eff_catch_here[code](C), eff_discharges(catch_clause, L).

eff_latent[flow](F, L, H) :- eff_latent[flow](G, L, H), resolves[code](C, G),
                             nearest_v[flow](F, C), not eff_discharged_at[code](C, L).

-- ...and the vocabulary the discharge table is over, kept closed. A label
-- nothing can discharge is fine; a discharge naming a label that is not one is
-- a row that removes nothing and says it removes something.
eff_discharge_unknown[audit](M, L) :- eff_discharges(M, L), not eff_label(L, _).

-- THE ONE INDEPENDENT ORACLE THIS LAYER HAS. `may_throw[code]` was built by
-- `w_exn_propagation` for the exception question alone — a different relation,
-- a different pack, written before this layer existed — and the `exn`
-- projection of the general machinery must reproduce it ROW FOR ROW. Both
-- directions, because one of them alone is satisfied by a rule that derives
-- nothing at all.
eff_exn_only[audit](F)   :- eff_latent[flow](F, exn, none), not may_throw[code](F).
may_throw_only[audit](F) :- may_throw[code](F), not eff_latent[flow](F, exn, none).

-- ...AND THE GATE THAT ORACLE IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO BE. `may_throw` carries
-- ONE LABEL, so a discharge written `not eff_catch_here(C)` on the whole arm —
-- swallowing the callee's whole row at a try instead of the one label a handler
-- removes — agrees with it EXACTLY. The oracle cannot see the difference,
-- because the difference is entirely in the seven labels the oracle does not
-- carry. Found by asking where the check cannot look rather than what else
-- could be broken, and closed here rather than left as a surviving mutant.
--
-- MEASURED: twelve (call, label) pairs in this corpus sit at a caught call site
-- with a non-`exn` label — `read<local>` and `alloc` at eight sites — so the
-- row is populatable and empty rather than empty because nothing reaches it.
eff_swallowed[audit](F, C, L, H) :- eff_catch_here[code](C), resolves[code](C, G),
                                    eff_latent[flow](G, L, H), L != exn,
                                    nearest_v[flow](F, C),
                                    not eff_latent[flow](F, L, H).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 4. NAMING A FUNCTION'S EFFECT — THE LEAST LANDMARK ABOVE ITS ROW
--
-- `effect_of[flow](F, Name)` is the smallest named row that contains F's, which
-- is the Moore-family closure the audits in section 1 certify exists and is
-- unique. A function with no labels at all gets `total`, which is a positive
-- answer rather than an absence — that is the whole reason the bottom is named.
--
-- THE COMPARISON IS `eff_leq` AND IT IS NOT MATERIALISED PER FUNCTION PAIR.
-- "Is this call at least as effectful as that one" is
-- `effect_of(F, NF), effect_of(G, NG), eff_leq(NG, NF)` — a two-join query
-- anybody can run. Writing it as a relation would be |functions| squared rows
-- for no answer the query does not already give, on a corpus of 395 functions
-- and an engine whose space wall is 500 000 rows.
eff_subject[flow](F)        :- fn_node[code](F).
eff_over[flow](F, N)        :- eff_name(N), eff_latent[flow](F, L, H), not eff_row(N, L, H).
eff_bounded[flow](F, N)     :- eff_subject[flow](F), eff_name(N), not eff_over[flow](F, N).
eff_bounded_low[flow](F, N) :- eff_bounded[flow](F, M), eff_lt(M, N).
effect_of[flow](F, N)       :- eff_bounded[flow](F, N), not eff_bounded_low[flow](F, N).

-- ...and the two ways that could be wrong, as rows. A function with no name is
-- a hole in the closure; a function with two is the ambiguity `join_ambiguous`
-- forbids arriving through a subject instead of through a pair.
eff_unnamed[audit](F)       :- eff_subject[flow](F), not effect_of[flow](F, _).
eff_two_names[audit](F, A, B) :- effect_of[flow](F, A), effect_of[flow](F, B), A != B.

-- THE JOIN, USED RATHER THAN DECORATED. A caller's effect must already contain
-- its callee's wherever nothing is discharged, so joining the two names must
-- give the caller's own back. Anything else is a propagation rule that has
-- dropped a label, and this is the one place the join operation is READ by
-- something other than a test.
eff_join_short[audit](F, G, J) :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C),
                                  not eff_catch_here[code](C),
                                  effect_of[flow](F, NF), effect_of[flow](G, NG),
                                  eff_join(NF, NG, J), J != NF.

-- WHERE A CALLEE IS STRICTLY PURER THAN ITS CALLER — the comparison put to work
-- on the corpus rather than left as a query in a comment. Bounded by the call
-- edges, so it costs what the call graph costs and nothing more.
eff_purer_callee[flow](C, F, G) :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C),
                                   effect_of[flow](F, NF), effect_of[flow](G, NG),
                                   eff_lt(NG, NF).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5. THE CONCRETE COLUMN — `http:get` RATHER THAN `io`
--
-- A concrete effect is a PAIR (Surface, Operation), rendered `surface:operation`
-- for a reader. facts/js-effects.rofl carries the argument for having two
-- columns and the provenance of each half; this section is the derivation.
--
-- THE SURFACE HALF IS DERIVED AND THE OPERATION HALF IS DERIVED. Neither is
-- authored anywhere in this tree, which is the constraint the founding finding
-- imposes: Koka's set is closed at eight and `http:get` opens an infinite one,
-- so a concrete effect that somebody TYPED would be the taxonomy being invented
-- after all.
--
-- WHAT IS DERIVABLE TODAY, with no surface pack at all: the builtin prototypes.
-- `prototype_of[flow]` reads the receiver's KIND — `[1,2,3].join(",")` needs
-- nothing carried as a value — and `builtin_prototype` names the six the
-- language owns. That gives ten call sites in this corpus with a surface and an
-- operation and no `.d.ts` involved.
eff_surface[flow](C, P) :- unresolved_call[code](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                           ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                           prototype_of[flow](O, P), builtin_prototype(P).
-- ...and the second source, which needs the pack the runtime and globals agents
-- are building: a free NAME whose surface something declares.
-- `ambient_binding` is empty today, so this arm derives nothing and is not
-- dead — it is the shape the surface plugs into, stated as a rule so that the
-- day the rows arrive nothing here has to change.
eff_surface[flow](C, S) :- unresolved_call[code](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                           ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O), ast_name[code](O, Name),
                           ast_node[code](C, _, File, _), ambient_binding(File, Name, S).

eff_operation[flow](C, Key) :- eff_surface[flow](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                               selects[flow](N, Key).
concrete_effect[flow](C, S, Op) :- eff_surface[flow](C, S), eff_operation[flow](C, Op).

-- `⊑`, AND IT IS ONE ROW PER MEMBER SUPPLIED BY THE SURFACE PACK. Nothing in a
-- `.d.ts` signature says `readFileSync` reads and `writeFileSync` writes; the
-- declaration FILE fixes the surface and says nothing about the effect. So this
-- is the one part of the column that is a table rather than a derivation, and
-- it is a table SOMEBODY ELSE OWNS — which is why an unattributed member is an
-- open cell below rather than a guess.
concrete_denotes[flow](S, Op, E) :- concrete_effect[flow](_, S, Op), ambient_effect(S, Op, E).
eff_here[flow](C, L, H)          :- concrete_effect[flow](C, S, Op),
                                    ambient_effect(S, Op, E), eff_row(E, L, H).

-- THE PREORDER THE MAP INDUCES, and it is named so that nobody mistakes it for
-- an order ON the concrete names. `http:get` and `fs:write` are incomparable
-- and always will be; what is comparable is what they DENOTE, and that is the
-- lattice doing the work the concrete column cannot.
--
-- IT RANGES OVER `concrete_denotes` AND NOT OVER `ambient_effect`, AND THE
-- CHANGE IS THE ARGUMENT SECTION 4 ALREADY MAKES ABOUT `eff_leq`, arriving one
-- relation over. That paragraph refuses to materialise the comparison per
-- FUNCTION PAIR — "|functions| squared rows for no answer the query does not
-- already give, on a corpus of 395 functions and an engine whose space wall is
-- 500 000 rows" — and this rule was |ambient_effect| squared. It was invisible
-- while `ambient_effect` was EMPTY: 0 x 0 is 0, and the shape of a rule is not
-- visible in its row count until something populates it.
--
-- MEASURED 2026-09-09 the day the surface arrived (w_effect_ambient_call): 571
-- `ambient_effect` rows produced about 330 000 here, and the world in
-- test/js-attrs.test.ts — which loads every JS pack in the tree — came back
-- `$rule(...)/space_exhausted` with eighteen of its nineteen tests red. Bounded
-- by `concrete_denotes` it is the pairs a CALL SITE in the corpus reaches, 17
-- over `w_js_ambient`, and that is also the only version anybody can ask a
-- question about: comparing two members nothing calls is a comparison about
-- nothing. The raw form is one query over `ambient_effect` for anyone who wants
-- it, exactly as `eff_leq` over `effect_of` is.
concrete_leq[flow](S1, O1, S2, O2) :- concrete_denotes[flow](S1, O1, E1),
                                      concrete_denotes[flow](S2, O2, E2), eff_leq(E1, E2).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE RESIDUE, AS A POSITIVE RELATION AND NOT AS AN AUDIT.
--
-- These are NOT `[audit]` and the distinction is the one CLAUDE.md records: a
-- gate red on an honest checkout gets switched off. Both of these are non-empty
-- today BY DESIGN — the surface pack does not exist — so they are the frontier,
-- countable and queryable, in exactly the shape `unresolved_call` and
-- `frontier_at` already have. What is asserted about them is that their
-- SURFACES are named, not that they are empty.
concrete_unmapped[flow](S, Op) :- concrete_effect[flow](_, S, Op),
                                  not ambient_effect(S, Op, _).
eff_call_unattributed[flow](C) :- unresolved_call[code](C, _),
                                  not concrete_effect[flow](C, _, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AND THE AUDITS THAT DO GO RED, all three about a surface pack that is wrong
-- rather than absent.
--
-- A `⊑` naming something that is not a landmark maps into nothing and
-- contributes nothing, silently — the same silence `orphan_claim` exists for,
-- arriving through a relation nobody in this tree has written yet.
concrete_unnamed[audit](S, Op, E) :- ambient_effect(S, Op, E), not eff_name(E).
-- A surface with no declared origin is a name somebody typed. The whole
-- argument for the concrete column is that its names come from a declaration
-- file; a surface that cannot say which file is the invented taxonomy the
-- founding finding refused.
concrete_no_origin[audit](S) :- ambient_effect(S, _, _), not surface_origin(S, _).
concrete_bad_origin[audit](S, O) :- surface_origin(S, O), not eff_origin(O).
-- ASYNC MUST NOT COME BACK AS A CONCRETE NAME. The founding finding refused
-- ASYNC as an effect on a measurement — `await` is transparent to value flow
-- and `yield` is not — and a concrete column is exactly where it would
-- reappear, as `promise:await` or `async:spawn`, mapping into nothing. A
-- concrete effect must denote a landmark; one that denotes `total` while
-- claiming to describe suspension is the smuggling this row names.
concrete_async_smuggled[audit](S, Op) :- ambient_effect(S, Op, E), eff_bot(E),
                                         eff_suspension_word(Op).
edb(eff_suspension_word).
eff_suspension_word(then).
eff_suspension_word(await).
eff_suspension_word(next).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5b. AN OPERATOR WHOSE SEMANTICS IS A CALL — w_effect_implicit_coercion
--
-- `a + b` runs `valueOf` or `Symbol.toPrimitive` on an operand that has one;
-- `-x` runs `valueOf`; `` `${x}` `` runs `toString`. This is `accessor_call`
-- wearing a third syntax — a construct whose surface is an operator and whose
-- semantics is a call — and it is the same shape `w_destructuring_hides_a_call`
-- found in a binding.
--
-- THE OPERAND DECIDES, NOT THE OPERATOR, and that is the whole mechanism. A
-- conversion method is a MEMBER of the operand's value, so:
--
--   a PRIMITIVE has no conversion method to run. `ToPrimitive` of a string, a
--     number, a boolean or a bigint is the identity, so `"a" + "b"` cannot call
--     anything user-written — a positive statement about the construct and the
--     same answer Koka gives for reading an immutable binding.
--   an IN-PROGRAM OBJECT that overrides `valueOf` or `toString` is a CALL, and
--     it propagates like any other call. `member_value[flow]` already answers
--     which key an object literal or a class declares, so this arm needed no
--     machinery of its own.
--   an IN-PROGRAM OBJECT that overrides NEITHER converts through
--     `Object.prototype`, which is the language's own and performs nothing. A
--     second positive statement, and the one a rule keyed on the operator alone
--     could not make.
--   ...and everything else is the RESIDUE, named rather than seeded. An operand
--     the value layer did not trace MAY be an object with an override, and the
--     honest answer is the one `eff_call_unattributed` already gives for an
--     unresolved call: a positive relation naming the frontier. Seeding the TOP
--     of the lattice instead was measured and refused — it takes
--     `eff_label_unseen` from two labels to none and makes `effect_of` `top` for
--     most of the corpus, which is the layer's sharpest statement destroyed to
--     say something nobody can use.
--
-- WHICH OPERATORS RUN NO USER CODE AT ALL, AS A LIST, AND THE REST BY
-- COMPLEMENT — the same shape section 2 of rules/js-modules.rofl uses for
-- `bare`, so an operator no rule below claimed converts rather than nothing.
-- The complement is the safe direction for a may-set.
--
-- EACH ONE IS ITS OWN RULE AND THE VALUE IS A CONSTANT, which is not style.
-- `attr_key_read_free` in rules/js-attrs.rofl reports a key some rule reads with
-- the VALUE LEFT FREE, and such a rule reads every operator there is — so
-- writing this as one rule over a table would silently switch
-- `unconsumed_value[audit]` off for `operator` and retire five era markers
-- nothing reads. Measured, not guessed: that audit is what
-- test/js-attrs.test.ts MUTANT 7 rests on.
eff_op_inspects[code](N) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "===").
eff_op_inspects[code](N) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "!==").
eff_op_inspects[code](N) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "typeof").
eff_op_inspects[code](N) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "void").
eff_op_inspects[code](N) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "!").

-- TWO OPERATORS WHOSE HIDDEN CALL IS NOT A CONVERSION, and this is the half the
-- item had to answer for before writing a rule. `x instanceof C` consults
-- `C[Symbol.hasInstance]` and `delete o[k]` performs a WRITE on top of
-- converting its key — neither is `ToPrimitive`, so neither is what the
-- mechanism above models. They are deliberately absent from `eff_op_inspects`,
-- which puts them in the converting complement: that over-reports a conversion
-- and never under-reports a call, which is the direction a may-set must err in.
-- MEASURED: `typeof` and `delete` have NO SITE in this corpus and `instanceof`
-- has none either, so this row is exercised by a probe in
-- test/js-effects.test.ts rather than by the fixtures.
eff_op_beyond[flow](N, has_instance) :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "instanceof").
eff_op_beyond[flow](N, delete_own)   :- ast_attr[code](N, operator, "delete").

eff_converts[code](N) :- ast_node[code](N, binary_expression, _, _), not eff_op_inspects[code](N).
eff_converts[code](N) :- ast_node[code](N, unary_expression, _, _),  not eff_op_inspects[code](N).

eff_coerced[flow](N, X) :- eff_converts[code](N), ast_child[code](N, left, 0, X).
eff_coerced[flow](N, X) :- eff_converts[code](N), ast_child[code](N, right, 0, X).
eff_coerced[flow](N, X) :- eff_converts[code](N), ast_child[code](N, argument, 0, X).
-- ...AND THE THIRD SYNTAX, WHICH HAS NO OPERATOR TO READ. `` `${x}` `` is
-- `ToString(x)` and a template with no interpolation converts nothing at all —
-- three of the seven templates in this corpus, and the difference is the
-- `expressions` child rather than a marker. `template_literal` is NOT an
-- allocation and that is settled in facts/js-effects.rofl: a string has no
-- identity to allocate, which is why this kind is open for COERCION and not
-- filed with `reg_exp_literal`.
eff_coerced[flow](T, X) :- ast_node[code](T, template_literal, _, _),
                           ast_child[code](T, expressions, _, X).

-- THE CONVERSION METHODS, AS KEYS. `Symbol.toPrimitive` is deliberately not
-- here: it is a COMPUTED key, and what spelling `key_name[code]` gives a
-- computed key is `w_computed_key_names`' open question rather than this
-- section's — guessing `"toPrimitive"` would be a well-formed question about a
-- real relation that nothing objects to, which is exactly the defect
-- `unpopulatable` is recorded as being blind to.
edb(eff_conv_key).
eff_conv_key("valueOf").
eff_conv_key("toString").

eff_conv_object[flow](N, X)     :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), may_be_node[flow](X, _).
eff_conv_call[flow](N, M)       :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), may_be_node[flow](X, O),
                                   eff_conv_key(Key), member_value[flow](O, Key, M).
eff_conv_overridden[flow](N, X) :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), may_be_node[flow](X, O),
                                   eff_conv_key(Key), member_value[flow](O, Key, _).
-- an in-program object overriding neither key converts through the language's
-- own prototype, which performs nothing
eff_conv_default[flow](N, X)    :- eff_conv_object[flow](N, X), not eff_conv_overridden[flow](N, X).
-- a primitive has no conversion method at all
eff_conv_primitive[flow](N, X)  :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), may_be_lit[flow](X, _),
                                   not eff_conv_object[flow](N, X).
-- ...and the frontier, in the shape `eff_call_unattributed` already has
eff_conv_untraced[flow](N, X)   :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), not may_be_lit[flow](X, _),
                                   not may_be_node[flow](X, _).

-- THE PROPAGATION, AND IT IS A SECOND ARM OF `eff_latent` RATHER THAN A ROW IN
-- `resolves`. Adding the edge to the call graph would move the call-graph
-- oracle, which is measured against V8's own stack frames and knows nothing
-- about a conversion; the effect of a conversion belongs to the effect layer
-- and the arm is written where it is read.
eff_latent[flow](F, L, H) :- eff_conv_call[flow](N, M), eff_latent[flow](M, L, H),
                             nearest_v[flow](F, N).

-- TOTALITY, IN THE SHAPE SECTION 6 OF rules/js-modules.rofl USES. Every coerced
-- operand is a primitive, an object or untraced, and never none of the three —
-- an identity, so it is true of any corpus rather than of this one.
eff_conv_accounted[audit](N, X)   :- eff_conv_primitive[flow](N, X).
eff_conv_accounted[audit](N, X)   :- eff_conv_object[flow](N, X).
eff_conv_accounted[audit](N, X)   :- eff_conv_untraced[flow](N, X).
eff_conv_unaccounted[audit](N, X) :- eff_coerced[flow](N, X), not eff_conv_accounted[audit](N, X).

-- ...AND THE OPERATOR TABLE MUST NOT NAME A NODE THAT IS NEITHER. `operator`
-- sits on five kinds — binary, unary, update, assignment and logical — and
-- `eff_op_inspects` reads the attribute without asking which, so a `!==` this
-- section believes it has classified could be sitting on a kind this section
-- does not model. Empty here and populatable: `=`, `+=`, `++` and `||` are all
-- in the corpus and none of them is a spelling in the list above.
eff_op_off_kind[audit](N, K) :- eff_op_inspects[code](N), ast_node[code](N, K, _, _),
                                K != binary_expression, K != unary_expression.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5c. AN IMPORT EVALUATES A MODULE — w_effect_module_evaluation
--
-- `import './m.js'` runs m's whole top level, so an import's effect is the
-- imported module's TOP-LEVEL effect. That is a question about the MODULE GRAPH
-- rather than about a node, and it is a MISSING EDGE rather than a missing
-- surface: the module is in the corpus and `import_target[code]` in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl already names it.
--
-- THE MODULE GRAPH IS MOSTLY BORROWED. `module_source[code]` already carries
-- `import_declaration`, `export_all_declaration` and the `export ... from` form
-- of `export_named_declaration`, and the specifier resolution below is the same
-- four literals `import_target` uses — a single-segment relative specifier
-- against `corpus_file`, with the kernel's own string destructors and no host
-- loan at all. What is NOT borrowed is stated where the copy is made, and it is
-- gated there. rules/js-modules.rofl answers the same question more completely
-- — it walks a real disk — and it is
-- NOT loaded here: this layer's world is the call corpus, whose five files
-- resolve by basename, and pulling in the modules pack would need the two blind
-- host emitters and would change nothing about the join.
--
-- THE ONE KIND `module_source` DOES NOT CARRY is the dynamic form, and it is
-- one arm.
eff_mod_src[code](N, Src, F) :- module_source[code](N, Src, F).
eff_mod_src[code](N, Src, F) :- ast_node[code](N, import_expression, F, _),
                                ast_child[code](N, source, 0, S), ast_value[code](S, Src).

-- ERASURE, AND BOTH MARKERS, because neither implies the other — the trap
-- rules/js-modules.rofl records twice and meets a third time here.
-- `import type { X } from './m'` marks the DECLARATION; `import { type X }`
-- marks the SPECIFIER and leaves the declaration saying "value". A declaration
-- ALL of whose specifiers are erased evaluates nothing, and one with NO
-- specifier (`import './m'`) evaluates everything — which is why the second arm
-- reads `eff_has_spec` rather than negating the first.
eff_type_only[code](I)  :- ast_attr[code](I, import_kind, "type").
eff_type_only[code](I)  :- ast_attr[code](I, export_kind, "type").
eff_has_spec[code](I)   :- eff_mod_src[code](I, _, _), ast_child[code](I, specifiers, _, _).
eff_value_spec[code](I) :- eff_mod_src[code](I, _, _), ast_child[code](I, specifiers, _, Sp),
                           not ast_attr[code](Sp, import_kind, "type"),
                           not ast_attr[code](Sp, export_kind, "type").
eff_erased[code](I)     :- eff_type_only[code](I).
eff_erased[code](I)     :- eff_has_spec[code](I), not eff_value_spec[code](I).

-- THE SPECIFIER RESOLVER, AND IT IS A COPY WITH A CONTROL RATHER THAN A REUSE.
-- `import_target[code]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl answers exactly this question
-- with the kernel's own string destructors and no host loan — and it is closed
-- over `module_source[code]`, which has NO ARM FOR THE DYNAMIC FORM. Reading it
-- here answered three of the four kinds and was SILENT about the fourth, which
-- is how this copy came to exist: measured, `import('./m.mjs')` derived nothing
-- at all and no audit said so, because the specifier never reached
-- `module_basename`.
--
-- SO THE FOUR LITERALS ARE WRITTEN AGAIN AND THE TWO RELATIONS ARE COMPARED.
-- `eff_mod_target_disagrees[audit]` asserts they agree ROW FOR ROW wherever
-- `module_source` carries the specifier, in both directions — which is what
-- makes this a copy with a gate rather than a second opinion, and it is the
-- same shape `eff_exn_only` / `may_throw_only` already have one section up.
eff_mod_basename[code](Src, Base) :- eff_mod_src[code](_, Src, _),
                                     Head is str_pre(Src, "/"), Head = ".",
                                     N is str_segs(Src, "/"), N = 2,
                                     Base is str_seg(Src, "/", 1).
eff_mod_target[code](Src, T) :- eff_mod_basename[code](Src, Base),
                                corpus_file[code](T), T = Base.

eff_mod_target_disagrees[audit](Src, T) :- import_target[code](Src, T),
                                           not eff_mod_target[code](Src, T).
eff_mod_target_disagrees[audit](Src, T) :- eff_mod_target[code](Src, T),
                                           module_source[code](_, Src, _),
                                           not import_target[code](Src, T).

eff_evaluates_at[code](I, T) :- eff_mod_src[code](I, Src, _), not eff_erased[code](I),
                                eff_mod_target[code](Src, T).

-- ...AND THE FRONTIER, as a positive relation rather than a gate. A specifier
-- naming a module this corpus does not contain has no module to ask, and that
-- is a missing FILE rather than a missing rule — the same row
-- `import_outside_corpus[audit]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl already reports, seen
-- from the effect side. It is the whole difference between this cell and
-- `call_expression`'s: add the file and the answer arrives with no rule change,
-- while nothing anybody can add to a corpus supplies `ambient_effect`.
eff_import_outside[flow](I, Src) :- eff_mod_src[code](I, Src, _), not eff_erased[code](I),
                                    not eff_mod_target[code](Src, _).

-- A MODULE'S OWN TOP LEVEL — the join over the FILE, which is the join over a
-- body one scope out. `nearest_v[flow]` ranges over functions only, so a node no
-- function encloses is a node the module body runs, and the negation is the
-- whole definition.
eff_in_fn[flow](N) :- nearest_v[flow](_, N).

eff_module[flow](F, L, H) :- eff_here[flow](N, L, H), not eff_in_fn[flow](N),
                             ast_node[code](N, _, F, _).
-- ...and a call written at the top level runs the callee's body there
eff_module[flow](F, L, H) :- resolves[code](C, G), not eff_in_fn[flow](C),
                             ast_node[code](C, _, F, _), eff_latent[flow](G, L, H).

-- THE SITE'S CONTRIBUTION, AND THE JOIN OVER THE GRAPH IS THE SAME UNION THE
-- JOIN OVER A BODY IS. An `import_declaration` is a top-level node, so its
-- `eff_here` row reaches `eff_module` through the arm above and the transitive
-- closure over the module graph needs no arm of its own — two rules with one
-- head are a union, which is what a join over a row IS.
--
-- **AND ES MODULES CAN BE CYCLIC, SO THIS IS A FIXPOINT AND NOT A WALK.**
-- `eff_here` -> `eff_module` -> `eff_here` is a POSITIVE cycle; the engine's
-- least fixpoint over it is the least solution, which for a may-set is exactly
-- the right answer and is what the language's own semantics says: a cyclic
-- module group is evaluated ONCE, every member's top level runs, and every
-- member therefore sees the whole group's effect. Nothing here assumes
-- acyclicity and nothing needs to. MEASURED: this corpus's module graph is
-- ACYCLIC — `beta -> alpha`, `beta -> gamma`, `gamma -> alpha`, `gamma -> delta`,
-- and gamma.mjs's own header says the direction was chosen to avoid a cycle —
-- so the cyclic case is exercised by a probe in test/js-effects.test.ts rather
-- than by the fixtures, and it is a probe rather than a sixth fixture because
-- test/js-modules.test.ts imports every `.mjs` in that directory with node.
--
-- THE SUSPENSION IS NOT SMUGGLED IN HERE. `import('./m')` returns a promise and
-- evaluates m; the effect is m's row and the suspension is CONTROL, exactly as
-- `await_expression` is waived under `a_suspension_is_not_an_effect`. There is
-- no `load` label and no ordering label, and `eff_import_invented[audit]` below
-- is the row that says a site contributes what its target has and nothing else.
eff_here[flow](I, L, H) :- eff_evaluates_at[code](I, T), eff_module[flow](T, L, H).

eff_import_invented[audit](I, L, H) :- eff_evaluates_at[code](I, T), eff_here[flow](I, L, H),
                                       not eff_module[flow](T, L, H).

-- NAMING A MODULE'S EFFECT — the same Moore closure section 4 runs over a
-- function, run over a file, because a module-graph reader wants a name and not
-- a row. `corpus_file[code]` is the subject, so a file with no top-level
-- operation gets `total` as a positive answer rather than as an absence.
eff_mod_subject[flow](F)        :- corpus_file[code](F).
eff_mod_over[flow](F, N)        :- eff_name(N), eff_module[flow](F, L, H), not eff_row(N, L, H).
eff_mod_bounded[flow](F, N)     :- eff_mod_subject[flow](F), eff_name(N), not eff_mod_over[flow](F, N).
eff_mod_bounded_low[flow](F, N) :- eff_mod_bounded[flow](F, M), eff_lt(M, N).
effect_of_module[flow](F, N)    :- eff_mod_bounded[flow](F, N), not eff_mod_bounded_low[flow](F, N).

eff_mod_unnamed[audit](F)         :- eff_mod_subject[flow](F), not effect_of_module[flow](F, _).
eff_mod_two_names[audit](F, A, B) :- effect_of_module[flow](F, A), effect_of_module[flow](F, B), A != B.

-- THE JOIN, USED RATHER THAN DECORATED, a second time. An importer's module
-- effect must already contain the effect of every module it evaluates, so
-- joining the two names must give the importer's own back — the same sentence
-- `eff_join_short[audit]` makes about a caller and its callee, made about a
-- module and the module it loads. This is the row a dropped label in the graph
-- closure would show up in.
eff_mod_join_short[audit](F, T, J) :- eff_evaluates_at[code](I, T), ast_node[code](I, _, F, _),
                                      effect_of_module[flow](F, NF), effect_of_module[flow](T, NT),
                                      eff_join(NF, NT, J), J != NF.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5d. A HIDDEN CALL THAT REACHES `calls` AND NOT `resolves`
--     w_destructuring_hides_a_call, 2026-09-09
--
-- THE LEDGER SAID THESE FOUR KINDS WERE ORDINARY CALLS HERE AND THREE OF THEM
-- CONTRIBUTED NOTHING. facts/js-effects.rofl reads *`pattern_iterates` and
-- `pattern_accessor` turn each into a `resolves` row, so at this layer they are
-- ordinary calls* — and only the first half is true. `pattern_accessor` reaches
-- `calls[code]` DIRECTLY and deliberately, because a rest over two getters is
-- two true calls at one node and `ambiguous_call[audit]` reads two `resolves`
-- at one node as an over-approximation. `eff_latent`'s second arm closes over
-- `resolves`. So the getter side of a destructuring form landed in the call
-- graph and nowhere in this layer.
--
-- MEASURED WITH A PROBE, four functions over one getter that writes and throws
-- — `get hot() { sink = sink + 1; throw ... }`:
--
--   gate.hot            (a member read)     read<local> write<local> exn alloc
--   const { hot } = gate                    NOTHING
--   const { ...rest } = gate                NOTHING
--   ({ ...gate })                           alloc only — the literal's own
--
-- ...and the same hole one hop further along the ITERATOR side, which the first
-- half does not cover either: `pattern_iterates` IS a `resolves` row and the
-- `next()` it implies is a `calls` row, so an iterator whose `next` writes is
-- invisible while the iterator method itself propagates. `pattern_next[code]`
-- in rules/js-controlflow.rofl exists so this section can NAME that callee
-- without copying a four-premise join.
--
-- **THIS SECTION MEASURES THE HOLE AND DOES NOT CLOSE IT.** The arm that would
-- close it is one line and section 5b already writes it — `eff_conv_call`
-- propagates a coercion's hidden callee exactly this way — and it is REFUSED
-- BY THE OWNER, on 2026-09-09, on a reason that is about the merge and not
-- about the model: the repair belongs to the call-graph and effect packs
-- together, and it is not to arrive inside a destructuring merge. Two other
-- branches found `may_throw` defects of the same shape on the same day and all
-- of them left the relation alone for the same reason.
-- `w_effect_reads_only_what_resolves` in facts/worklist.rofl owns it.
--
-- AND THE REFUSAL CARRIES A NUMBER, which is what separates a decision from an
-- omission. The arm was written and priced before it was reverted: with it in,
-- `eff_latent` over the five shared fixtures is 401 rows and without it 401 —
-- gained 0, lost 0, because every getter in this corpus is pure — so the repair
-- costs the honest tree nothing and is worth exactly one probe. The test plants
-- it and reports that, rather than leaving the reader to wonder.
--
-- WHAT IS BUILT INSTEAD IS THE DEMONSTRATION. `eff_hidden_call[flow]` names
-- every door that reaches `calls[code]` without a `resolves[code]` row, and
-- `eff_edge_unclosed[flow]` below reports what those doors DROP. Neither
-- contributes a label to anything, so `eff_latent` is untouched BY
-- CONSTRUCTION and not by luck.
eff_hidden_call[flow](N, M) :- pattern_accessor[code](N, M).
eff_hidden_call[flow](N, M) :- pattern_next[code](N, M).

-- ...AND THE RESIDUE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT. `eff_join_short[audit]` is the
-- gate that says a caller's row contains its callee's, and IT READS `resolves`
-- — THE SAME RELATION THE PROPAGATION READS. A gate that looks where the
-- propagation looks cannot see an edge the propagation missed, which is why
-- this hole survived the layer being built, audited and closed, and why it is
-- still green today in the very world where the rows below are non-empty. That
-- sentence, and not the missing arm, is the finding.
--
-- IT IS A POSITIVE RELATION AND NOT AN `[audit]`, for the reason
-- `eff_call_unattributed` and `concrete_unmapped` are: it reports a hole that
-- is DELIBERATELY UNREPAIRED, and an audit red on an honest tree gets switched
-- off. What it reports is every door that reaches `calls[code]` without a
-- `resolves[code]` row — the destructuring getter side, the pattern's `next`,
-- and `for_of_statement`'s own second hop, which reaches `calls` from
-- rules/js-callgraph.rofl by exactly the same route.
--
-- AND IT IS EMPTY ON THIS CORPUS, WHICH IS A FACT ABOUT THE FIXTURES AND NOT
-- ABOUT THE RULE — measured, not assumed. Every getter in the five fixtures is
-- pure and every `next` they reach is `bump`, which has no operation to
-- propagate, so there is nothing for a row to carry. Two probes in
-- test/js-effects.test.ts supply what the corpus cannot: a getter that writes
-- and throws, and an iterator whose `next` writes. Both make these rows appear
-- WHILE `eff_join_short[audit]` STAYS EMPTY IN THE SAME WORLD.
--
-- MODULE-LEVEL CALLERS ARE OUT OF SCOPE BY A PREMISE, not by accident: a call
-- written at a module's top level contributes to `eff_module[flow]`, which is a
-- different relation with a different subject, so `fn_node[code](F)` is what
-- keeps this row about functions.
eff_edge_closed[flow](F, G) :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_fn[code](F, C).
eff_edge_closed[flow](F, G) :- resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C).

eff_edge_unclosed[flow](F, G, L, H) :- calls[code](F, G), fn_node[code](F),
                                       not eff_edge_closed[flow](F, G),
                                       eff_latent[flow](G, L, H),
                                       not eff_latent[flow](F, L, H).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 6. WHERE THIS PACK CANNOT LOOK, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER
-- 6. WHAT RUNS WHEN A CLASS IS DEFINED, AND WHAT RUNS WHEN ONE IS BUILT
--    2026-09-09 — queue item w_effect_class_initialisers.
--
-- `r_effect_latent` is the rule these five kinds are NOT, and saying how they
-- differ is the whole item. That rule says a function definition performs no
-- operation: what its body does happens at a CALL, and `eff_latent`'s second
-- arm carries the row over a `resolves` edge. THESE FIVE ARE THE OPPOSITE CASE
-- — code that runs at a moment other than a call — so the edge `eff_latent`
-- rides does not exist for them:
--
--   class C { static { f(); } }    f runs where the DECLARATION stands
--   class C { static x = f(); }    f runs where the DECLARATION stands
--   class C { x = f(); }           f runs at every `new C()`, and never at all
--                                  if nothing ever constructs a C
--
-- TWO MOMENTS, TWO CARRIERS, AND THE ITEM'S NOTE IS HALF RIGHT ABOUT THE CALL
-- SITE. It says the propagation "has no call site to hang it on"; that is true
-- of the DEFINITION half and false of the CONSTRUCTION half. `may_be_node[flow]`
-- already answers which class a `new` expression constructs, so an instance
-- initialiser's row hangs on the `new` exactly as a callee's row hangs on a
-- call. Only the definition half needed an edge of a new shape, and it goes to
-- the DECLARATION NODE — which is what makes `class_declaration` a kind with a
-- contribution of its own rather than the twenty-sixth container.
--
-- AND THE JOIN IS STILL FREE. Two rules with one head are a union, so each
-- carrier below is a join over its parts with no join operation written
-- anywhere — the same reason `eff_latent` needs none over a body. Feeding both
-- back into `eff_here` means a class written inside a function reaches that
-- function through `nearest_v` with no arm of its own, and the labels that walk
-- already delivered arrive a second time into a SET, which is idempotent.

edb(eff_class_form).
-- ONE ROW, AND `class_expression` IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE SECOND. Its cell is
-- `r_effect_closure` and belongs to another item; a rule of this one firing on
-- it would answer a kind this item does not own. THE REFUSAL IS PRICED rather
-- than asserted: `eff_define_unreached[flow]` below is exactly what it loses,
-- one row on this corpus, and adding `eff_class_form(class_expression)` is the
-- whole change on the day somebody takes it.
eff_class_form(class_declaration).

edb(eff_field_kind).
-- THE THREE KINDS THAT CARRY AN INITIALISER. `class_method` and
-- `class_private_method` are not here: they are function definitions and are
-- filed with the latent group, where they belong.
--
-- `class_accessor_property` IS HERE, AND ITS SYNTHESISED PAIR ADDS NO LABEL.
-- `accessor a = 1` declares a private backing slot, a getter that reads it and
-- a setter that writes it; on this layer's question the getter's read of the
-- slot is a `read` on the same receiver that `o.a` is already a `read` of, and
-- the setter's write is the `write` that `o.a = v` already is. So what the kind
-- contributes BEYOND a plain field is nothing, and what it contributes AS a
-- field is its initialiser at construction time. That is the reverse of the
-- control-flow layer's answer for the same kind, where the synthesised call is
-- the whole content of the verdict — the two layers are asking different
-- questions of one construct, which is the pattern this model is built on.
eff_field_kind(class_property).
eff_field_kind(class_private_property).
eff_field_kind(class_accessor_property).

eff_class_body[code](CD, P) :- eff_class_form(K), ast_node[code](CD, K, _, _),
                               ast_child[code](CD, body, 0, B),
                               ast_child[code](B, body, _, P).
eff_field_value[code](CD, P, V) :- eff_class_body[code](CD, P), eff_field_kind(K),
                                   ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                                   ast_child[code](P, value, 0, V).

-- THE TWO MOMENTS, AND BOTH ARMS ARE POSITIVE ON PURPOSE. Writing the second as
-- `not ast_attr(P, static, true)` would make the pair total by construction and
-- default every field the scanner stops flagging to construction time, silently
-- — which is the wrong direction, because a static initialiser reported as
-- per-construction runs at a moment nothing in the model then names. Two
-- positive arms leave `eff_moment_unplaced[audit]` able to bite, and that audit
-- is the only thing in this tree that would notice the attribute going away.
--
-- A FIELD WITH NO INITIALISER IS IN NEITHER, and `blank;` in the fixture is the
-- site that says so: babel emits the property with no `value` child, so
-- `eff_field_value` derives nothing for it and there is no moment to place. The
-- member exists at run time and holding `undefined` performs no operation.
eff_field_moment[flow](P, definition)   :- eff_field_value[code](_, P, _),
                                           ast_attr[code](P, static, true).
eff_field_moment[flow](P, construction) :- eff_field_value[code](_, P, _),
                                           ast_attr[code](P, static, false).

eff_define_part[flow](CD, V)    :- eff_field_value[code](CD, P, V),
                                   eff_field_moment[flow](P, definition).
eff_construct_part[flow](CD, V) :- eff_field_value[code](CD, P, V),
                                   eff_field_moment[flow](P, construction).

-- A STATIC BLOCK IS THE SECOND DEFINITION-TIME PART, and it carries no `static`
-- attribute at all — measured: a `static_block` node has an empty `ast_attr`,
-- the word is in its KIND and not in its facts. So it cannot ride the arms
-- above and gets one of its own.
eff_define_part[flow](CD, S) :- eff_class_body[code](CD, S),
                                ast_node[code](S, static_block, _, _).

-- ...AND THREE MORE THINGS RUN AT DEFINITION, none of which is a field:
--   the `extends` expression   `class B extends mountOf() {}` calls `mountOf`
--                              before the class object exists
--   a COMPUTED KEY             `class C { [k()]() {} }` evaluates `k()` when the
--                              class is defined, whether or not the member it
--                              names is ever reached
--   a DECORATOR                `@deco class C {}` calls `deco` at definition,
--                              which the control-flow layer already files as
--                              `definition_time_call`
--
-- THE DECORATOR ARMS DO NOT TOUCH `decorator`'S OWN CELL, which is
-- `r_effect_transfer` and stays: all six decorator nodes in this corpus have a
-- `resolves` row, so the transfer verdict was never the missing part. What is
-- added here is WHEN, which is the only thing this item is about.
eff_define_part[flow](CD, S)  :- eff_class_form(K), ast_node[code](CD, K, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](CD, super_class, 0, S).
-- THE COMPUTED-KEY ARM IS TWO RULES AND A DOUBLE NEGATION, AND IT WAS ONE RULE
-- AND A POSITIVE TEST UNTIL `attr_blind_guard[audit]` SAID SO IN ONE RUN.
-- `ast_attr[code](P, computed, true)` beside `ast_child[code](P, key, 0, KN)` is
-- a positive attribute guard, and a positive attribute test cannot reach a kind
-- that carries no such attribute at all — which here is
-- `class_private_method` and `class_private_property`, because babel emits no
-- `computed` field for a private member. The two rows are not a defect in what
-- this rule DERIVES (a private name can never be a computed key, so the answer
-- is the same either way); they are a statement about what the guard can REACH,
-- which is `f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute`
-- and the reason `key_name` in rules/js-structure.rofl is written with a
-- negation.
--
-- SO THIS IS WRITTEN WITH THE NEGATION TOO, one relation over: `eff_plain_key`
-- holds of every member the `computed` flag does not mark, INCLUDING every kind
-- that has no such flag, and the arm below negates it. Same extension, no
-- blindness to declare — and declaring it was not available, because
-- `attr_slot_gap_ok` is keyed by (attribute, position, kind) and an excuse
-- written for this rule would have silently excused `key_name`'s guard as well,
-- disarming MUTANT 9 in test/js-attrs.test.ts.
eff_plain_key[code](P)        :- eff_class_body[code](_, P),
                                 not ast_attr[code](P, computed, true).
eff_define_part[flow](CD, KN) :- eff_class_body[code](CD, P), not eff_plain_key[code](P),
                                 ast_child[code](P, key, 0, KN).
eff_define_part[flow](CD, D)  :- eff_class_form(K), ast_node[code](CD, K, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](CD, decorators, _, D).
eff_define_part[flow](CD, D)  :- eff_class_body[code](CD, P),
                                 ast_child[code](P, decorators, _, D).

-- WHAT RUNS WHEN A PART IS REACHED, AND IT STOPS AT A FUNCTION. This is
-- `nearest_v` said from the other end, and it is the same two-rule linear walk
-- for the reason rules/js-dataflow.rofl records beside that relation: the
-- "descendant with no function in between" form written as a negation over a
-- quadratic was 35 per cent of a fixpoint when it was tried there.
--
-- THE PART ITSELF IS IN THE SET, AND THE SECOND ARM KEEPS A FUNCTION NODE WHILE
-- REFUSING TO DESCEND THROUGH IT. That is what makes
-- `static forge = (n) => hammered(n)` contribute `alloc` and NOT whatever
-- `hammered` does: the closure is allocated when the class is defined and its
-- body is latent until something calls it. Dropping the guard would file every
-- latent body under the definition moment, which is the exact confusion the
-- latent group exists to prevent.
eff_runs_in[flow](P, P) :- eff_define_part[flow](_, P).
eff_runs_in[flow](P, P) :- eff_construct_part[flow](_, P).
eff_runs_in[flow](P, X) :- eff_runs_in[flow](P, Y), not fn_node_v[flow](Y),
                           ast_in[code](Y, X).

-- A TRY INSIDE A CLASS PART CATCHES WHAT RUNS IN THAT PART, and `caught_here`
-- is STRUCTURALLY UNABLE to say so — which is a hole the rules above open and
-- therefore one they close. `caught_here` asks `nearest_v(F, TS)` and
-- `nearest_v(F, N)` for one F, because a try catches what runs in its own
-- FUNCTION; a `static { try { risky(); } catch {} }` at a module's top level has
-- no such F, `try_of` is empty for it, and the handler catches nothing as far as
-- the model is concerned. MEASURED, not suspected: with the carriers in and this
-- arm out, a class whose static block wraps a throwing call in a try/catch
-- reported `exn` at definition time.
--
-- The same equality one level over: `eff_runs_in` for the part is what
-- `nearest_v` is for the function, so the two literals below are `caught_here`'s
-- two literals with the walk swapped, and `try_catches` is the same table.
eff_catch_here[code](N) :- eff_runs_in[flow](P, TS),
                           ast_node[code](TS, try_statement, _, _),
                           in_try_block[code](TS, N), eff_runs_in[flow](P, N),
                           try_catches[code](TS).

-- THE TWO CARRIERS. Each is a join over its parts (arm one) closed over the
-- call graph (arm two), and arm two is `eff_latent`'s propagation with
-- `eff_runs_in` where `nearest_v` stands — which is the one substitution the
-- whole item comes down to. `not eff_discharged_at` is carried across
-- unchanged, so a `try` inside a static block removes `exn` from the class's row
-- and leaves the seven other labels where they are.
class_define_eff[flow](CD, L, H)    :- eff_define_part[flow](CD, P),
                                       eff_runs_in[flow](P, X), eff_here[flow](X, L, H).
class_define_eff[flow](CD, L, H)    :- eff_define_part[flow](CD, P), eff_runs_in[flow](P, C),
                                       resolves[code](C, G), eff_latent[flow](G, L, H),
                                       not eff_discharged_at[code](C, L).
class_construct_eff[flow](CD, L, H) :- eff_construct_part[flow](CD, P),
                                       eff_runs_in[flow](P, X), eff_here[flow](X, L, H).
class_construct_eff[flow](CD, L, H) :- eff_construct_part[flow](CD, P), eff_runs_in[flow](P, C),
                                       resolves[code](C, G), eff_latent[flow](G, L, H),
                                       not eff_discharged_at[code](C, L).

-- A SUBCLASS RUNS ITS ANCESTORS' INSTANCE INITIALISERS, INCLUDING THE SHADOWED
-- ONES. The base constructor evaluates the base's initialiser and the derived
-- class then overwrites the value, so BOTH initialisers run and only one value
-- survives. That is why this walks `super_of` directly rather than reading
-- `inherited_field[flow]`, which subtracts an own key on purpose: right for a
-- value, wrong for an effect. `Doubloon` declares `static pick` over `Coin`'s
-- and is the site that separates the two readings.
class_construct_eff[flow](CD, L, H) :- super_of[flow](CD, SD),
                                       class_construct_eff[flow](SD, L, H).

-- THE TWO SEEDS, AND THEY ARE WHY THE CELLS CLOSE. The first gives the
-- declaration node its own row, so a class defined inside a function reaches
-- that function through `nearest_v` and a class at a module's top level is at
-- least queryable — the join from a top level into an importer is
-- w_effect_module_evaluation's, and this is the row it will read. The second is
-- the construction edge, and it is one the CALL GRAPH does not have: a call in a
-- field initialiser has no enclosing function, so `encloses` attributes it to
-- nobody, and V8 agrees — it names the frame `<instance_members_initializer>`.
eff_here[flow](CD, L, H) :- class_define_eff[flow](CD, L, H).
eff_here[flow](X, L, H)  :- ast_node[code](X, new_expression, _, _),
                            may_be_node[flow](X, CD), class_construct_eff[flow](CD, L, H).

-- AND EVALUATING A CLASS DECLARATION ALLOCATES — one row in the table section 2
-- already keeps, not a rule. `class_expression` has been in that list since the
-- layer opened and `class_declaration` was not, and the difference between them
-- is not in the language: both create a fresh constructor with a fresh, mutable
-- `prototype`, which is the same fresh mutable identity the other six rows name.
--
-- WHY `function_declaration` STAYS OUT WHILE THIS GOES IN, since the two look
-- alike and are filed apart. A function declaration is HOISTED: its object
-- exists before the first statement of its scope runs, so nothing is performed
-- where it is written. A class declaration sits in the temporal dead zone until
-- control reaches it and the object is created THERE. This layer's carrier is
-- per-node, so "at this node" is the question being asked, and the two kinds
-- answer it differently.
eff_alloc_kind(class_declaration).

-- THE IDENTITY THAT SAYS THE TWO MOMENTS WERE NOT COLLAPSED. A field
-- initialiser runs at definition or at construction, never both and never
-- neither — true of any corpus rather than of this one, which is the same shape
-- `eff_member_both` has for the read/write partition one section up. A COUNT of
-- definition-time parts could not tell a static field the rule missed from an
-- instance field it filed under the wrong moment.
eff_moment_both[audit](P, A, B) :- eff_field_moment[flow](P, A),
                                   eff_field_moment[flow](P, B), A != B.
eff_moment_unplaced[audit](P)   :- eff_field_value[code](_, P, _),
                                   not eff_field_moment[flow](P, _).

-- ...AND THE RESIDUE OF REFUSING TO WIDEN INTO `class_expression`, as a POSITIVE
-- relation and not an audit, for the reason `concrete_unmapped` is one: it is
-- non-empty on an honest checkout by design, and a gate red on an honest
-- checkout gets switched off. One row today — `export const Bracket = class
-- extends mountOf() {}` — and it is the entire price of `eff_class_form` having
-- a single row.
eff_define_unreached[flow](CE, S) :- ast_node[code](CE, class_expression, _, _),
                                     ast_child[code](CE, super_class, 0, S).
eff_define_unreached[flow](CE, P) :- ast_node[code](CE, class_expression, _, _),
                                     ast_child[code](CE, body, 0, B),
                                     ast_child[code](B, body, _, P),
                                     ast_node[code](P, static_block, _, _).
eff_define_unreached[flow](CE, V) :- ast_node[code](CE, class_expression, _, _),
                                     ast_child[code](CE, body, 0, B),
                                     ast_child[code](B, body, _, P),
                                     eff_field_kind(K), ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                                     ast_attr[code](P, static, true),
                                     ast_child[code](P, value, 0, V).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 7. WHERE THIS PACK CANNOT LOOK, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER
--
-- 1. NO IMPLICIT THROW OF ANY KIND. `exn` is seeded from `throw_statement` and
--    nothing else, exactly as `may_throw` is, so a `null.x`, an
--    out-of-scope name and a failed coercion are all `total` here. That is a
--    limit of the seed and not of the lattice, and it is the same one
--    facts/js-statements.rofl already records for strict mode.
-- 2. NO TERMINATION ARGUMENT. Every loop is `div`, including the ones that
--    obviously stop. The may-set is wide in the safe direction.
-- 3. THE HEAP IS TWO-VALUED AND ONE OF THE TWO IS "THE VALUE LAYER LOST IT".
--    `global` here means untraceable, which is a superset of the actual global
--    heap: a receiver that came in as a parameter is `global` although it may
--    well be a local object of the caller's. Narrowing it is inter-procedural
--    value flow, which is a different item.
-- 4. THE JOIN HAPPENS AT THE FUNCTION BOUNDARY, NOT AT EVERY NODE. `eff_latent`
--    reads `nearest_v`, so the row of a BLOCK or an `if` is not materialised.
--    Every question this layer has been asked is about a function or a call, so
--    the per-node rows would be cost with no reader — and if one ever wants
--    them, they are one rule over `ast_within` and a large one.
-- 5. `eff_purer_callee` COMPARES NAMES AND NOT ROWS. Two functions whose rows
--    are incomparable both round UP to the same landmark and compare equal, so
--    this relation under-reports. It is the price of naming, and the raw
--    comparison over `eff_latent` is available to anybody who wants it.
-- 6. A COERCED OPERAND THE VALUE LAYER LOST CONTRIBUTES NOTHING, AND IS NAMED.
--    `eff_conv_untraced[flow]` is 141 of 369 conversion sites here. The wide
--    answer — the top of the lattice — was measured and refused: it takes
--    `eff_label_unseen` from two labels to none and makes `effect_of` `top` for
--    most of the corpus, which is the same reason `unresolved_call` does not
--    seed anything either. So this layer UNDER-reports a coercion, in the one
--    direction a may-set is not supposed to err, and says so here rather than
--    leaving it to be found.
-- 7. A CONVERSION REACHES ONLY `valueOf` AND `toString`. `Symbol.toPrimitive`
--    is a COMPUTED key and what spelling `key_name[code]` gives one is
--    `w_computed_key_names`' open question; and an `Array.prototype.toString`
--    converts its ELEMENTS in turn, which this layer does not follow.
-- 8. A MODULE'S ROW IS JOINED AT THE FILE BOUNDARY AND A CONVERSION IS NOT.
--    `eff_module` reads `eff_here` at the top level and `eff_latent` through a
--    top-level `resolves` edge; the conversion arm of section 5b propagates
--    through `nearest_v`, so a coercion written at a module's TOP LEVEL whose
--    operand overrides `valueOf` reaches no function and no module. There is no
--    such site in this corpus and it is one rule if there ever is.
-- 6. AN INSTANCE INITIALISER REACHES ITS ENCLOSING FUNCTION TWICE, and one of
--    the two arrivals is early. `nearest_v` walks a function down through a
--    class body into a NON-static field initialiser, because `class_property`
--    is not a value context — so a function that merely DEFINES a class already
--    carries that initialiser's labels although only a `new` runs them.
--    Section 6's construction edge is the arrival that is timed correctly; the
--    other is a may-set widening in the safe direction, and narrowing it means
--    changing `nearest_v`, which is the value layer's relation and the call
--    graph's caller attribution.
-- 7. `may_throw[code]` HAS NO CONSTRUCTION EDGE, so the oracle in section 3 and
--    the rule in section 6 disagree in principle and agree on this corpus.
--    `function g() { return new C(); }` with `class C { x = boom(); }` throws,
--    `eff_latent(g, exn, none)` now says so, and `may_throw(g)` cannot: its
--    propagation rides `resolves` + `nearest_v`, and the call in the initialiser
--    has no enclosing function to be the caller. No field initialiser in these
--    five fixtures reaches a throw, so `eff_exn_only[audit]` is empty here — and
--    test/js-effects.test.ts plants the shape that makes it fire, because an
--    agreement that has never been asked the question is not evidence.
--    `f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge` and it is `may_throw` that is
--    wrong, not the projection.

-- WHAT THIS PACK'S LEDGERS READ, written out because the kernel stopped
-- emitting a bridge row for a rule that licenses itself by existing. Seven
-- crossings and every one of them is a rule in this file: [code] joins
-- `nearest_v[flow]` and `member_node_v[flow]`, [flow] reads the scanner's book
-- and the label tables in [main], and every audit here reads both books it
-- audits. Some of these are already declared by js-callgraph and js-dataflow;
-- they are repeated because a pack that cannot be loaded on its own without
-- leaking is a pack whose crossings live somewhere else.
imports(code, main).
imports(code, flow).
imports(flow, code).
imports(flow, main).
imports(audit, code).
imports(audit, flow).
